<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!-- The health notice board on the hall's far wall: the one thing in the 2009
     view that is not a person, a sofa or a wall, and the reason a clinic reads
     as a public room rather than a corridor.

     It is a GUI cell, not a model cell, and the difference is not styling. A
     poster is a flat rectangle whose entire content is an image, which is what
     the format's own picture cell carries: a name, a file and a measure, with
     no .dae, no .dep and no lighting to author. Placed as a model it would also
     arrive signed, because the renderer reads any cell over 0.6 m tall as a room
     and hangs its name near the ceiling; a notice board does not need a name
     when it is already covered in one.

     The measure travels as pixel scale, the format's only size unit for a
     picture: the map is 512 px square, so 3.4 mm per pixel is the 1.74 m board
     a person reads from three metres away. -->
<poster-cell>
    <name>Health Notices</name>
    <metadata/>
    <image-uri>wla://wonderland-outpatient-clinic/art/notices.png</image-uri>
    <pixelScaleX>0.0034</pixelScaleX>
    <pixelScaleY>0.0034</pixelScaleY>
    <position-component>
        <!-- On the wall the visitor meets on the way in, left of the hall's own
             name sign and above the waiting sofas, which is where the reference
             has it. The hall's inner face is at z = -8.0, so -7.85 hangs the card
             15 cm off the plaster: coplanar with the wall it shows, and the wall
             wins the depth test. Yaw 0 turns its +Z normal into the room. -->
        <translation><x>-3.5</x><y>1.75</y><z>-7.85</z></translation>
        <rotation><x>0.0</x><y>1.0</y><z>0.0</z><angle>0.0</angle></rotation>
    </position-component>
</poster-cell>
